 Answering "give me the work with hash X".

 A quotation resolves from the local store and nowhere else. The design's order
 is local store, then a known peer, then a registry, and the last two do not
 exist -- but the reason they do not is not that the client is hard. It is that
 there has never been anything on the other end. Nothing in this tree serves a
 work to anyone. This is the other end.

 The verb is by-hash and not the sync offer, deliberately (Damian, 2026-07-16).
 `Trust chapter FactSync` can already reconcile two stores, but that is bulk: the
 asker offers everything it holds and the answerer replies with everything the
 offer did not name. Fetching ONE work through it means publishing your whole
 store to ask a single question and serving your whole store to whoever asks. A
 quotation names one hash. So the ask is one hash and the answer is one work.

 What this chapter does NOT do, and must not be quietly extended to do: it does
 not decide who may ask. Every work in the store is offered to any caller that
 names its hash. That is a real policy and it is the right one HERE, because a
 hash is not a name you can guess -- you cannot ask for a work you have not
 already been told the digest of, and being told the digest is how a quotation
 gets written in the first place. Serving by-hash is not serving the store.

 Nothing here signs anything, and nothing needs to. The reply is answerable by
 its own content: the asker hashes what came back and checks it against the hash
 it asked for. A peer that lies is caught by the addressing, not by trust. That
 is the same property that lets the import gate treat the transport as untrusted,
 and it is why this is a small chapter rather than an architecture.

 We say:

Section: Serving

 The index is built once by the caller and handed in. Rebuilding it per request
 would re-read the whole log for every quotation.

  serve-work : WorkIndex, AgentIdentity, WorkRequestMsg -> WorkReplyMsg
  serve-work (ix) (me) (req) =
    when work-by-hash ix (req.hash)
      is None -> WorkReplyMsg { from = me, hash = req.hash, payload = "" }
      is Just (d) -> WorkReplyMsg { from = me, hash = req.hash, payload = (when source-def-to-text d is Just (t) -> t is None -> "") }

 An empty payload means "not here", and it is not an error. A peer that does not
 hold a work is the ordinary case; the asker tries the next one.

  reply-is-empty : WorkReplyMsg -> Boolean
  reply-is-empty (r) = text-length (r.payload) == 0

Section: Asking

 The request. There is nothing to it, which is the point of choosing this verb.

  ask-work : AgentIdentity, Text -> WorkRequestMsg
  ask-work (me) (hash) = WorkRequestMsg { from = me, hash = hash }

Section: Taking An Answer

 The only part of this exchange that matters for safety.

 A reply is NOT trusted because of who sent it. It is decoded, and the work it
 carries is hashed, and that hash must equal the hash that was asked for.
 Anything else is refused -- a peer that sends a different work, a corrupted
 work, or a work it made up is caught here by arithmetic rather than by
 reputation. This is what makes an untrusted transport safe to use: the same
 argument the import gate makes about the `%%QUOTED-WORKS%%` blob.

 It checks the reply's own hash field too. A reply that names a different hash
 than the one requested is answering a question nobody asked.

  accept-reply : WorkReplyMsg, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  accept-reply (reply) (wanted) =
    if reply.hash /= wanted then None
    else if text-length (reply.payload) == 0 then None
    else accept-decoded (source-def-from-text (reply.payload)) wanted

  accept-decoded : Maybe SourceDefinition, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  accept-decoded (m) (wanted) =
    when m
      is None -> None
      is Just (d) -> if fact-content-hash (d.sd-content) == wanted then Just d else None

 What accept-reply does NOT do is decide whether the work is TRUSTED. It proves
 the peer answered the question honestly; whether the author is somebody you
 admit is the gate's question, and the gate already asks it -- signature, pinned
 key, trust floor. Content addressing and trust are two checks and this chapter
 owns exactly one of them. Conflating them is how a transport ends up deciding
 what to believe.
